{meta: title Old Home Place}
{meta: artist J.D. Crowe and the New South}
{meta: composer Dean Webb and Mitchell Jayne}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
It's been [A]ten long [C#7]years since I [D]left my [A]home
In the hollow where I was [E]born
Where the [A]cool fall [C#7]nights make the [D]wood smoke [A]rise
And the fox hunter [E]blows his [A]horn
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
I [A]fell in [C#7]love with a [D]girl from the [A]town
I thought that she would be [E]true
[A]I ran [C#7]away to [A][D]Charlottesville
And worked in a [E]sawmill [A]too
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
[E]What have they done to the old home [A]place
[B7]Why did they tear it [E]down
And [A]why did I [C#7]leave my plow [D]in the [A]field
And look for a [E]job in the [A]town
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
Well the [A]girl ran [C#7]off with [D]somebody [A]else
The tariffs took all my [E]pay
[A]And here I [C#7]stand where the old [D]home [A]stood
Before they [E]took it [A]away
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
Now the [A]geese fly [C#7]south and the cold [D]wind [A]moans
As I stand her and hang my [E]head
[A]I've lost my [C#7]love I've [D]lost my [A]home
And now I [E]wish that I was [A]dead 
{end_of_verse}

